## What went wrong first {#sec:wrong}

The first fits all failed, and the failures are worth a paragraph. With the plan's vacuum monomial $M=Q_1P^3$ and the assumption that the kernel is $\zeta$-independent, no level of the ladder fit, not even the full jet space $\{J^2_aJ^3_bJ^3_c\}$ with 1295 unknowns (training residual $4\cdot10^{-2}$, hold-out error $8\cdot10^2$). The cause was normalisation: $p_{02}=A_sQ_1$ with $A_s=(\zeta_3-\zeta_2)e^{\sigma\theta_s}\Pi'(\theta_s)$ is $\zeta$-dependent, and so is the frame-tie scalar. Once one divides by $c$ and uses $M=P^3p_{02}$, the kernel is exactly $\zeta$-free. Likewise $\langle x_0^G|\Psi_B\rangle/n_B$ turns out to be, to $2\cdot10^{-77}$, a constant times $Q_1(\theta_1)P^3(\theta_1)Q_1(\theta_2)P^3(\theta_2)$ (exponent $1$ on each, $0$ on the other ten numbers), so the "x$_0$-free" target $T(B)=\langle\Phi_\infty|\Psi\rangle D_B(\omega_0)/\langle\Omega_0\otimes\Omega_0|\Psi\rangle$ is not constant times $Q_{\rm num}$ but $c_T(\zeta)\,Q_{\rm num}M_1M_2$: the two targets are the same fit with one free scalar per $\zeta$ point. And the one-site-fixed-$a$ limit is wrong, as said above. The vacuum monomial of the plan was off by a factor $5/4$ ($M_{\rm vac}=2h^2W'P^3$, not $\tfrac52h^2W'P^3$), and the twelve candidate monomials reduce to five.
